===Noun===
{{en-noun|+|pl1qual=dated}}

# {{lb|en|equestrianism}} A [[rein#Noun|rein]] of a [[horse#Noun|horse]] [[hold#Verb|held]] [[loosely]] so that the [[animal#Noun|animal]] can [[move#Verb|move]] more [[freely]]; also, the [[free#Adjective|free]] [[condition#Noun|condition]] that this [[give#Verb|gives]] to a horse.
#* {{RQ:Sewell Black Beauty|page=113|passage=I needed no whip, or spur, for I was as eager as my rider: he saw it, and giving me a '''free rein''', and leaning a little forward, we dashed after them.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1884|author=Elizabeth [Platt] Karr|chapter=The Leap—the Standing Leap—the Flying Leap|title=The American Horsewoman|location=Boston, Mass.&#59; New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Houghton Mifflin Harcourt|Houghton, Mifflin and Company]]{{nb...|New York: 11 East Seventeenth Street; The Riverside Press, Cambridge [Mass.]}}|page=256|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/americanhorsewom00karr/page/256/mode/1up|oclc=616615|passage=[I]n the rise during the leap, just previous to the spring, no efforts whatever must be made by the rider to support the horse, or to lift him, but instead, she should simply hold the reins so lightly that his mouth can just be felt, which is called "giving a '''free rein'''."}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1908|author=w:Virgil|chapter=Book I|translator=[[w:Theodore Chickering Williams|Theodore C[hickering] Williams]]|title=[[w:Aeneid|The Æneid of Virgil Translated into English Verse]]|location=Boston, Mass.&#59; New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Houghton Mifflin Harcourt|Houghton Mifflin Company]]{{nb...|The Riverside Press Cambridge [Mass.]}}|year_published=[1910]|page=8|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/neidvirgil00virggoog/page/8/mode/1up|oclc=313514|passage=So ceased the sea's uproar, when its grave Sire / Looked o'er th' expanse, and, riding on in light, / Flung '''free rein''' to his winged obedient car.}}
# {{lb|en|figurative}} The [[absence]] of [[constraint]]s; [[freedom]] to [[act#Verb|act]], [[decide]], or [[speak#Verb|speak]].
#: {{synonyms|en|[[blank check]], [[blank cheque]]|carte blanche|free hand|proxy in blank}}
#* {{RQ:Quarles Virgin Widow|page=1|passage=[H]e vvas pleas'd to lend / '''Free reines''' to mirth, and to ſuſpend thoſe cares / That claime ſuch intereſt in th' Imperiall brovv.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1790|author=Thomas Pennant|chapter=[Chaucer]|title=Of London|location=London|publisher={{...|Printed for}} Rob[er]t Faulder,{{nb...|No. 42, New Bond Street.}}|page=47|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/b28778121/page/47/mode/1up|oclc=1326029917|passage=[[w:Geoffrey Chaucer|{{quote-gloss|Geoffrey}} Chaucer]] gave a '''free rein''' to his poetical mirth.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=9 August 1861|author=w:Wendell Phillips|editor=[[w:William Lloyd Garrison|W[illia]m Lloyd Garrison]]|title=Speech of Wendell Phillips, Esq. at the celebration of W. I. emancipation|journal=[[w:The Liberator (newspaper)|The Liberator]]|location=Boston, Mass.|publisher=J[ames] B[rown] Yerrinton & Son,{{nb...|printers.}}|volume=XXXI|issue=32 (number 1598 overall)|page=1|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/sim_liberator_1861-08-09_31_32/mode/1up|column=6|issn=2997-7649|oclc=1728160|passage=If you have any influence, therefore, on members of Congress, on editors, on the creators of public opinion, on your neighbors, on the rank and file of your army, teach them that with Massachusetts bayonets, it is better to be insubordinate, and shoot a Colonel, than it is, unasked, unauthorized, and Heaven-damned, to turn themselves into hunters of slaves. (Loud and prolonged cheers.) Help the Government to dare to give '''free rein''' to the ardor of the people.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1913|author=John B. O’Connor|entry=Ignatius of Constantinople, Saint|editors=Charles G[eorge] Herbermann; [[w:Edward A. Pace|Edward A[loysius] Pace]]; [[w:Condé Benoist Pallen|Condé B[enoist] Pallen]]; [[w:Thomas Joseph Shahan|Thomas J[oseph] Shahan]]; [[w:John J. Wynne|John J[oseph] Wynne]]|title=[[w:Catholic Encyclopedia|The Catholic Encyclopedia: An International Work of Reference on the Constitution, Doctrine, Discipline, and History of the Catholic Church{{nb...|[...] Fifteen Volumes and Index [...] Special Edition under the Auspices of the Knights of Columbus Catholic Truth Committee}}]]|edition=special|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=The Encyclopedia Press|volume=VII|page=647|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/catholicencyclop07herbuoft/page/646/mode/1up|oclc=191121193|passage=At the suggestion of the latter {{quote-gloss|{{w|Bardas}}}}, [[w:Michael III|Michael {{quote-gloss|III}}]] sought the assistance of [[w:Ignatius of Constantinople|Ignatius {{quote-gloss|of Constantinople}}]] in an effort to force [[w:Theodora (wife of Theophilos)|Theodora]] to enter a convent, in the hope of securing for himself an undivided authority and a '''free rein''' for his profligacy. The patriarch indignantly refused to be a party to such an outrage. Theodora, however, realizing the determination of her son to possess at any cost an undivided rule, voluntarily abdicated.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=2018|month=December|author=James Lambert|title=Setting the Record Straight: An In-depth Examination of ''Hobson-Jobson''|editor=Robert Lew|journal=w:International Journal of Lexicography|location=Oxford, Oxfordshire|publisher=w:Oxford University Press|volume=31|issue=4|page=487|doi=10.1093/ijl/ecy010|issn=0950-3846|oclc=644768428|passage=Such hedging is necessitated by the lack of in-depth knowledge of the contents, which also gives '''free rein''' to the scripting of unsubstantiated factoids concerning the book.}}
#* {{RQ:Guardian|date=21 July 2021|author=Phil Gates|title=Country diary: this forgotten grassland is a butterfly Serengeti|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20221219121100/https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2021/jul/21/country-diary-this-forgotten-grassland-is-a-butterfly-serengeti|passage=If I give my imagination '''free rein''' on this sultry afternoon, I could almost believe this shoulder-high grassland&nbsp;– a shimmering sea of false oat, cock’s-foot and meadow foxtail&nbsp;– is African savanna.}}
#* {{quote-web|en|date=6 August 2021|author=A. A. Dowd|title=The {{w|Ryan Reynolds}} action-comedy ''{{w|Free Guy}}'' is a ''{{w|Truman Show}}'' for the ''{{w|Fortnite}}'' age|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20230419014750/https://www.avclub.com/the-ryan-reynolds-action-comedy-free-guy-is-a-truman-sh-1847429238|work=w:The A.V. Club|passage=''Free City'' is, by design, a generic multiplayer sandbox—it's supposed to look like any and every '''free-rein''' video game metropolis.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en-GB|author=David Smith|title=Trump hints Musk ‘Doge’ team has free rein with Pentagon next in line for cuts|work=The Guardian|date=2025-02-07|url=https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/feb/07/trump-elon-musk-doge-agency|issn=0261-3077|passage=Trump suggested that he has given the tech billionaire '''free rein''' and appeared blase about the details.}}

